Description: Windows-style desktops, on the screen and over Remote Desktop
 Each account has one desktop, which runs whether or not anyone is looking at
 it, as on Windows. The machine's own screen (the "Patrone Desktop" login
 session) and Remote Desktop clients are both views of it, and whoever
 connects last has it: a Remote Desktop sign-in takes the screen back to the
 login screen, and signing in on the screen disconnects the Remote Desktop
 client.
 .
 Windows' Remote Desktop Connection and other RDP clients sign in with the
 account's own username and password, which PAM checks on every connection;
 nothing keeps a copy of them. Only accounts in the remote-desktop group may,
 and only after an administrator turns it on: with "Enable Remote Desktop" in
 Settings' System > Remote Desktop > Remote Login (Patrone's build of GNOME
 Settings), or "patrone-remote-desktop enable".
